---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro'
import EmojiCombiner from '../components/islands/EmojiCombiner'
import { graph, organization, webApplication, faqPage, breadcrumb } from '../lib/schema'

const path = '/emoji-combiner/'
const title = 'Emoji Combiner & Combo Generator — Merge Two Emojis'
const description =
  'Combine emojis online: overlay or mix any two emojis into one image and download it as a PNG. Free emoji combiner and mixer — works on iPhone, Android and desktop.'

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Can you combine two emojis?',
    a: 'Yes. Pick any two emojis above, choose Overlay or Side-by-side, adjust size and position, and download the merged result as a PNG. It works with every standard emoji, not just a fixed list of pairs.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is this different from Google’s Emoji Kitchen?',
    a: 'Emoji Kitchen serves hand-drawn mashups for specific pairs inside Gboard and Google Search. This combiner works in any browser — including on iPhone and desktop — lets you mix any pair, and gives you a downloadable PNG. Read more in our <a href="/emoji-kitchen/">Emoji Kitchen guide</a>.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I combine emojis on iPhone?',
    a: 'iPhones only mix emojis natively through Genmoji on devices that support Apple Intelligence (iOS 18.2 or later). On any iPhone, you can use this page in Safari instead — no app or iOS version required.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I use the combined emoji as a sticker or custom emoji?',
    a: 'Yes. The downloaded PNG can be uploaded as a custom emoji or sticker in Discord, Slack, Telegram, WhatsApp and most other chat apps. For platform-specific sizes, try the <a href="/slack-emoji-maker/">Slack</a> or <a href="/discord-emoji-maker/">Discord</a> makers.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the emoji combiner free?',
    a: 'Completely free, with no sign-up and no watermark. The compositing happens locally in your browser, so your creations are never uploaded anywhere.',
  },
]

const schema = graph(
  organization(),
  webApplication({
    path,
    name: 'Emoji Combiner',
    description,
    features: [
      'Merge any two emojis into one image',
      'Overlay and side-by-side layouts',
      'Adjustable size, position and opacity',
      'PNG download at 640×640',
      'Works on iPhone, Android and desktop browsers',
    ],
  }),
  faqPage(path, faqItems.map(({ q, a }) => ({ q, a: a.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, '') }))),
  breadcrumb([{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Emoji Combiner', path }]),
)
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} schema={schema}>
  <main class="page container">
    <section class="hero">
      <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › Emoji Combiner</nav>
      <h1>Emoji Combiner: The Free Emoji Combo Generator</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        An emoji combo maker and emoji mixer in one: put two emojis together — 🐱 + 🔥, 💀 + ☕,
        anything — and download the mashup as a PNG. No app, no Gboard, no sign-up.
        Looking for combos to copy as text instead? Those are on the
        <a href="/emoji-combinations/">emoji combinations</a> pages.
      </p>
    </section>

    <EmojiCombiner client:load />

    <section>
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
        <h2>Combine emojis in three steps</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><b>Choose your pair.</b><span>Tap the first slot and pick an emoji, then the second — or hit <em>Random pair</em> for instant chaos.</span></li>
        <li><b>Arrange the mashup.</b><span>Overlay one emoji on the other and drag the sliders to move, scale and fade it, or switch to side-by-side.</span></li>
        <li><b>Download the PNG.</b><span>Your combined emoji exports at 640×640 with a transparent background, ready for chats, memes and custom emoji uploads.</span></li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="prose prose-page">
      <h2>When to reach for the combiner</h2>
      <p>
        Combined emojis say things single emojis can't: a crying cat in the rain, a party avocado,
        coffee-powered skull energy. People use emoji mashups as reaction images, Discord and Slack
        custom emojis, story stickers and profile accents.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you want a fully custom face instead — choosing the eyes, mouth and brows yourself —
        use the <a href="/">Emoji Maker</a>. If you're after Google's official pre-drawn mashups,
        here's <a href="/emoji-kitchen/">how Emoji Kitchen works and where it's available</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Overlay or side-by-side: which layout to pick</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Overlay</strong> is the mashup mode. One emoji sits on top of the other, and the
        size, position and opacity sliders decide how much of the base shows through. It works best
        when the two emojis have different silhouettes — a face plus a small object (🐱 + 🎩,
        💀 + ☕) reads instantly, because the object lands as an accessory rather than a collision.
        Two round faces overlaid on each other usually turn to mush.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Side-by-side</strong> keeps both emojis whole and legible. Use it when the pairing
        is the joke rather than the drawing — 🐟 next to 🚲, or two characters reacting to each
        other. It also survives being shrunk to 28 pixels far better than an overlay does, which
        matters for chat emoji.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything is composited in your browser on a canvas, then exported at 640×640 with a
        transparent background. Nothing is uploaded, so the emoji you build never leaves your
        device.
      </p>

      <h2>Upload sizes for each platform</h2>
      <p>
        The 640×640 PNG is deliberately larger than any chat platform needs, so you can downscale
        without losing edges. These are the sizes and limits worth knowing before you upload:
      </p>
      <div class="spec-table-wrap card">
        <table class="spec">
          <thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Size</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Discord</td><td>128 × 128</td><td>256KB per emoji; 50 slots on a free server</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Slack</td><td>128 × 128</td><td>128KB per emoji</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Microsoft Teams</td><td>128 × 128</td><td>Custom emojis are set by the workspace admin</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Twitch</td><td>112, 56 and 28</td><td>All three sizes required, 1MB each</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Telegram / WhatsApp</td><td>512 × 512</td><td>Uploaded as stickers, not emojis</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        For emojis sized and named correctly on upload, the dedicated
        <a href="/slack-emoji-maker/">Slack</a>, <a href="/discord-emoji-maker/">Discord</a>,
        <a href="/teams-emoji-maker/">Teams</a> and
        <a href="/twitch-emote-maker/">Twitch</a> makers export the right dimensions directly.
      </p>

      <h2>How this compares to Emoji Kitchen, Genmoji and emojimix</h2>
      <p>
        There are four ways to end up with a merged emoji, and they are good at different things:
      </p>
      <div class="spec-table-wrap card">
        <table class="spec">
          <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Pairs available</th><th>Runs on</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>This combiner</td><td>Any two emojis</td><td>Any browser</td><td>Transparent PNG you keep</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Emoji Kitchen</td><td>147,000 pre-drawn</td><td>Gboard, Google Search</td><td>Hand-drawn sticker</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Genmoji</td><td>Generated from text</td><td>iPhone 15 Pro and newer</td><td>Apple sticker, on-device</td></tr>
            <tr><td>emojimix</td><td>~50,000 pre-drawn</td><td>Any browser</td><td>Sticker image</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The trade-off is coverage versus polish. Emoji Kitchen results look better than anything a
        compositor can produce, because a designer drew each one — but only the pairs Google chose
        exist, and the pair you want often is not among them. This combiner has no such list: every
        emoji works with every other emoji, and you control the framing. Genmoji goes further still
        by inventing new artwork, at the cost of needing recent Apple hardware — see
        <a href="/how-to-use-genmoji/">how to use Genmoji</a> for whether your device qualifies.
      </p>

      <h2>Combining emojis on iPhone and Android</h2>
      <p>
        Neither platform merges emojis natively in the way people expect. On Android, Gboard's Emoji
        Kitchen suggests mashups above the keyboard while you type, but only for supported pairs.
        On iPhone there is no keyboard integration at all — Genmoji is the closest equivalent and it
        requires an iPhone 15 Pro or newer running iOS 18.2 or later.
      </p>
      <p>
        This page sidesteps both. It runs in Safari and Chrome on any phone, needs no install, and
        gives you a file rather than a suggestion — which is the part that matters if you want to
        upload the result as a custom emoji somewhere.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Faq items={faqItems} />

    <section class="related">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Keep creating</p>
        <h2>More free emoji tools</h2>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/"><b>Emoji Maker</b><span>Build a custom emoji from 100+ Fluent-style parts, with SVG export.</span></a></li>
        <li><a href="/emoji-kitchen/"><b>Emoji Kitchen Guide</b><span>What it is, how to get it, and what to use when you don't have Gboard.</span></a></li>
        <li><a href="/emoji-combinations/"><b>Emoji Combinations</b><span>Copy-paste aesthetic, cute and funny emoji combos.</span></a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
